<spell-notebook "flag theming">
	<header level="1">
		Flag
		<header>A flag is used to represent a political state.</>
	</>
	<flag-tab-bar showing="theming"/>
	<section>
		<menu class="ui-docs-section-tabs" appearance="segmented" aria-label="Sections">
			<item link="#tokens">Tokens</>
			<item link="#a-flag-font">A flag font</>
			<item link="#themes">Themes</>
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Tokens">
		<markdown>
			Theme with the public `--ui-flag-*` tokens: set one on the element, on any ancestor (a region, `:root`), or on
			the flag's box through `::part(flag)`. They inherit into the shadow root, where the sheet reads each through a
			private alias, so nothing inside blocks them.

			- `--ui-flag-size-small` ... `-massive` are the size ratios; `--ui-flag-distance` the space after a flag.
			- `--ui-flag-font-family` is the font flags draw with; it defaults to the colour emoji fonts,
			  `--ui-font-family-emoji`. Windows' Chrome / Edge need a flag font: "A flag font", below.
			- Never set the `--_ui-*` names: they're the sheet's private plumbing.
		</>
		<markdown>Edit a value below to try it.</>
		<html>
			<ui-docs-tokens family="ui-flag" playground>
				<ui-text paragraph>
					<ui-flag country="fr"></>
					<ui-flag country="us"></>
					<ui-flag country="gb"></>
					<ui-flag country="de"></>
					<ui-flag country="jp"></>
					<ui-flag country="br"></>
					<ui-flag country="in"></>
					<ui-flag country="za"></>
					<ui-flag country="eu"></>
					<ui-flag country="un"></>
				</>
			</>
		</>
	</>

	<section label="A flag font">
		<markdown>
			Flags are emoji, drawn by the reader's emoji font. Mac, iPhone, Android, ChromeOS, Linux (Noto Color Emoji)
			and Firefox everywhere draw them. **Windows' Chrome and Edge don't:** Microsoft's emoji font, Segoe UI Emoji,
			has no country flags (Windows 10 or 11), so 🇫🇷 shows as the letters "FR", and England / Scotland / Wales
			as a plain black flag. Rainbow, transgender and pirate flags work everywhere.

			A page that wants flags there loads a flag font itself; `spell-ui` ships no font bytes:

			- Which font: "Twemoji Country Flags", through the `country-flag-emoji-polyfill` package (about 77 KB of
			  woff2, per its package; not measured here). Country flags only.
			- How: call `polyfillCountryFlagEmojis()` once. It checks whether the browser can draw flags, and only if it
			  can't (Windows Chromium) adds an `@font-face` for the font, fetched from a CDN by default. Elsewhere it
			  does nothing, and nothing is downloaded.
			- Then name the font first in `--ui-flag-font-family`, once for the page, in front of the emoji fonts.
			  `<ui-flag>` reads it, and so do the flags in `<ui-dropdown>` and `<ui-select>` rows.
			- Without it, nothing breaks: Windows readers see the two letters.
		</>
		<markdown>
			```js
			// the page's own module script, once
			import { polyfillCountryFlagEmojis } from "country-flag-emoji-polyfill"
			polyfillCountryFlagEmojis()
			```
		</>
		<markdown>
			```css
			/* the page's stylesheet:  the flag font first, then the emoji fonts it falls back to */
			:root {
			  --ui-flag-font-family: "Twemoji Country Flags", var(--ui-font-family-emoji), var(--ui-font-family);
			}
			```
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Themes">
		<markdown>
			Flags are emoji only, by design, so a theme leaves them as they are;  the theme menu in the header
			still applies a theme to the whole site.  Fomantic's `famfamfam` sprite theme isn't ported.
		</>
	</>
</spell-notebook>
